"Homo sapiens (ID 357250) - BioProject - NCBI (https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/bioproject/357250)\nciteturn8search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.9 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Accession: PRJNA357250 ID: 357250 ... Accession  | PRJNA357250; GEO: GSE92327\n\nAccession: PRJNA357250 ID: 357250\n\n## Homo sapiens (human)\n\n### Illumina bead array -/+ UV ASCC3 long isoform shRNA knockdown cells\n\nThis project is a component of the UV-Irradiation Induces a Noncoding RNA that Functionally Opposes the Protein Encoded by the Same Gene\n\nNavigate Across\n\n4 additional projects are components of the UV-Irradiation Induces a Noncoding RNA that Functionally Opposes the Protein Encoded by the Same Gene.\n\n115740 additional projects are related by organism.\n\nThe transcription-related DNA damage response was analyzed on a genome-wide scale with great spatial and temporal resolution. More...\n\nThe transcription-related DNA damage response was analyzed on a genome-wide scale with great spatial and temporal resolution. Upon UV irradiation, a slowdown of transcript elongation and restriction of gene activity to the promoter-proximal ~25 kilobases is observed. This is associated with a shift from expression of long mRNAs to shorter isoforms, incorporating alternative last exons (ALEs) that are more proximal to the transcription start site. Notably, this includes a shift from a protein-coding ASCC3 mRNA to a shorter transcript isoform of which the RNA, rather than an encoded protein, is critical for the eventual recovery of transcription. The protein-coding ASCC3 isoform counteracts the function of the non-coding isoform, indicating crosstalk between them. Thus, the ASCC3 gene expresses both coding and noncoding transcript isoforms with opposite effects on transcription recovery after UV-induced DNA damage Overall design: MRC5VA non-targeting shRNA control and MRC5VA ASCC3 long isoform (NM_006828.3) targeting shRNA cells were left untreated or UV irradiated 15 J/m2, followed by 20 hours recovery. RNA extracted from 3 biological replicates per sample were analyzed on Illumina Human HT-12 bead expression array. Less...\n\nAccession  | PRJNA357250; GEO: GSE92327\nData Type  | Transcriptome or Gene expression\nScope  | Multiisolate\nOrganism  | Homo sapiens[Taxonomy ID: 9606]Eukaryota; Metazoa; Chordata; Craniata; Vertebrata; Euteleostomi; Mammalia; Eutheria; Euarchontoglires; Primates; Haplorrhini; Catarrhini; Hominidae; Homo; Homo sapiens\nPublications  | Williamson L et al., \"UV Irradiation Induces a Non-coding RNA that Functionally Opposes the Protein Encoded by the Same Gene.\", Cell, 2017 Feb 23;168(5):843-855.e13\nSubmission  | Registration date: 13-Dec-2016\nSvejstrup Lab, The Francis Crick Institute\nRelevance  | Medical\n\nProject Data:\n\nResource Name  | Number\nof Links\n--- | ---\nPublications\nPubMed  | 1\nPMC  | 1\nOther datasets\nGEO DataSets  | 1\n\nGEO Data Details\n\nParameter  | Value\n--- | ---\nData volume, Spots  | 567876\nData volume, Processed Mbytes  | 17\nData volume, Supplementary Mbytes  | 3--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMessage 365508 - Python tracker (https://bugs.python.org/msg365508)\nciteturn8search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.6 years ago; Crawled: last month; Message365508 ... This issue tracker has been migrated to GitHub, and is currently read-only.\n\nMessage365508\n\nImage ➜\n\nThis issue tracker has been migrated to GitHub, and is currently read-only.\nFor more information, see the GitHub FAQs in the Python's Developer Guide.\n\nAuthor  | aeros\nRecipients  | aeros, pitrou, tomMoral, vstinner\nDate  | 2020-04-01.20:44:40\nSpamBayes Score  | -1.0\nMarked as misclassified  | Yes\nMessage-id  | <1585773880.56.0.94753969765.issue39812@roundup.psfhosted.org>\nIn-reply-to  | \nContent\n---\n    \n    I attached a PR to bpo-40115 to address the refleak in test_asyncio: PR-19282.\n---------------------------------------------------------------------------